{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785236491,"nonce":"xHujoTzmMhnIj2xRp57HfrLUFWk=","title":"ADR-0062: the change set drops the metadata its own walk already read, so the fold stats every path twice","message":"`changeset::read_change_set` walks the `overlayfs` upper layer and `lstat`s every entry to classify it — that is how it distinguishes a whiteout (a character device with `rdev == 0`) from a regular file, and a directory from a symlink. It then returns `Written` and `Directory` values carrying **paths only**, discarding the mode, mtime and size it just read.\n\nSo `settle::settle_change_set` has to `lstat` every written path and every directory **again** to build the tree entries. Two full stat passes over the change set where one would do.\n\nWorse than the cost is that it makes the metadata's *source* implicit. The ADR never states where a **directory's** mode and mtime come from in a merged view; the fold answers \"the upper wins\", which is correct but is a decision re-derived at the second stat rather than recorded at the first.\n\nFound while implementing s4's fold (`crates/scarab-server/src/settle.rs`).\n\n- [ ] Have `Written` and `Directory` carry the `mode`/`mtime_ms`/`size` the classifying walk already read\n- [ ] Drop the fold's second stat pass, and let the metadata source be a field rather than a re-derivation\n- [ ] Keep the ordering property: the walk reads metadata *before* anything is folded, so a Step that is still running cannot change what the change set records mid-fold","files":null}]}